# Workload generation

When the user attaches `docs/AI_AGENT_INSTRUCTIONS.md` and asks to generate workloads, follow that file. The chat only needs a `Generate workloads ...` line and, for a VM run, one real `ssh -i <key> root@<IPv4>` line. Extra sentences are optional.

This generator tree is the only template. Do not read or copy any other `TEMPLATE_*` directory. No `Legacy:` line means legacy mode is off. Do not refresh the VM unless the prompt explicitly confirms a refresh.

`through`, `to`, and `-` are inclusive. Repositories are created in the parent of this generator, even when Cursor's open folder is this generator. On the VM they install only under `/opt/benchmarks/<Workload Number>-<Repo Base Name>`. Do not use `/root` or `/opt/workloads`. Every `run_benchmark.sh` run appends to `/var/opt/benchmarks/runtime_ledger.csv`. Generation also installs the operator tools (`get_benchmark_results.sh`, `list_runs.sh`, `show_metrics.sh`) in the folder above the project root, as README.md describes. They are expected output, not scratch: leave them there and do not report them as files outside the project root. Generation also writes a bundle root into the project root itself: `README.md`, `get_benchmark_results.sh`, `list_runs.sh`, `run_benchmark_suite.sh` and `show_metrics.sh` (TEMPLATE_00_145). The scripts match `user_scripts/`. The README is filled in from `templates/suite-tools/BUNDLE_README_TEMPLATE.md` for the one platform whose workloads are in the folder; when the folder holds more than one platform, the README says so and does not pick one. An existing file there that differs is kept. These files are expected output, not scratch.

The only generation action is to save the chat text as the submitted-prompt artifact, then run from this generator directory:

```bash
python3 scripts/generation_host_preflight.py --prompt-file ../AI_AGENT_PROMPT_SUBMITTED_<WORKLOADS>_<UTC_TIMESTAMP>.md
```

Then run the start command that preflight prints, exactly as printed. It has this form, with the interpreter preflight chose in place of `<python>`:

```bash
MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1 <python> scripts/run_from_prompt.py --prompt-file ../AI_AGENT_PROMPT_SUBMITTED_<WORKLOADS>_<UTC_TIMESTAMP>.md
```

Run both from the generator directory in Git Bash (Windows) or bash. Preflight itself only needs Python 3 to start (`python3`, `python`, or `py -3`). When that interpreter lacks Python 3.10+ or PyYAML, preflight tries `GENERATOR_PYTHON` (if set), then `python3`, `python` and `py -3` on `PATH`, then `/c/Python/python.exe`, `/c/Python3*/python.exe` and `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Python\Python3*\python.exe`, and prints the start command with the first one that has both. If none does, it prints the `pip install pyyaml` command to run first. Do not type `python3` into the start command yourself. `MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1` stops Git Bash from rewriting paths. Save the prompt in the project root (the generator's parent) and pass it as the relative `../` path, never a `C:\...` or `/c/...` path.

Do not pass `--remote-root`, `--project-root`, or `--template-root`. Do not reimplement copy, setup, or smoke. Continue until every requested workload is recorded.

While the batch runs, wait once per workload: each wait returns on the **Successful time** line, a `[FAIL]` line, or `[RECORD] <N>` where N is the next workload not yet recorded (never a later one), or on process exit. Match the literal line text: a regular expression with the brackets escaped once (`\[RECORD\] 117` for workload 117) or the fixed string `[RECORD] 117`. A wrongly escaped pattern never matches; in TEMPLATE_00_141 one agent's first wait sat 30 minutes while 101–116 were already in the file. Such a wait may run up to 30 minutes. When a wait ends without a match (a timeout), read `batch_progress.log` itself, continue from the last `[RECORD]` line in it, and only then start the next wait; never repeat the same wait unread (TEMPLATE_00_142). A plain `sleep` with no trigger must not exceed 120 seconds. Report as soon as the batch finishes. Waiting for any `[RECORD]` returns at once on lines already printed; 120-second waits drew Cursor's repeated-poll warning, and one agent then waited for the last workload's record and missed three before it (TEMPLATE_00_139). A batch of 101–132 that finished at 05:32 was reported 21 minutes later because the agent was inside one long sleep (TEMPLATE_00_135). Wait on `batch_progress.log` in the project root, not on the batch console: start `tail -n +1 -F batch_progress.log` in a second background terminal right after the batch starts and wait on that terminal's output. The file gets every `[RECORD]`, workload `[FAIL]` and **Successful time** line, and a final `[DONE]` line when the batch process exits. Cursor stops showing a command's output after about 1,000 lines, so the batch console can freeze while the batch keeps running (TEMPLATE_00_140: 101–132 froze on workload 114 and 201–232 on 214); the progress file stays under about 60 lines. During a long workload it also gets every fifth heartbeat (`[<N> setup+smoke] still running …`, every 5 minutes), so while the batch is alive it never stays unchanged for more than about 6 minutes (TEMPLATE_00_142; in TEMPLATE_00_141 it was silent for 16 minutes during 431). If it has not changed for 15 minutes, read `batch_generation_manifest.json` (updated at every record) and check that the batch process is still running.

Helper scripts you write after the batch, for a review or a question, go in `/tmp` and are deleted when you finish; leave nothing in the project root or a repository.

A locked overlay is a fingerprint, not a ban. When smoke fails inside a file listed in `results/overlay_lock.json`, write the same bytes in `implementation_components/<component-id>/files/<path>`, the sibling path, and that path's `sha256`, then recheck with `create_one_workload.py --validate-only`. Do not leave the workload failed when those three copies can match. Do not replace the overlay with a new collector. Do not run `create_generated_repo.py` again on a non-empty sibling. `scripts/metric_contract.py` has no lock entry: repair the generator copy and the sibling copy, then use the same recheck. `Locked overlay rewritten` means the sibling and the digest disagree. `Implementation component mismatch` means the sibling and the template source disagree.

Log every change to a generator file during a batch with `python3 scripts/log_generator_repair.py --workload <N> --file <path> --reason "<why>"`. The batch summary ends with that list. A collector reports a counter the host does not have as `na (expected: <reason>)`; do not add new `classify_na` matches.
